CORE
JmlInstanceMethod
--function "JmlInstanceMethod.clamp:(III)I" --jml-source src/main/java/JmlInstanceMethod.java --unwind 5 -cp target/classes
^EXIT=0$
^SIGNAL=0$
^VERIFICATION SUCCESSFUL$
--
^warning: ignoring
^CONVERSION ERROR
^.*FAILURE$
--
Regression for JML on instance methods (non-static).

The bug:
  jml_lowering's positional-match loop iterated `params[i]` against
  `param_names[i]`. For instance methods, `params[0]` is the
  implicit `this` parameter; `param_names[0]` is the first
  user-declared parameter. So `x` was bound to the `this` slot,
  `lo` to the first int slot, etc. Equality between the int
  return value and the (now pointer-typed) `x` triggered:

    Invariant check failed
    File: solvers/flattening/boolbv_equality.cpp:18
    Reason: equality_types_match

The fix:
  Detect whether `params[0]` is `this` and apply a +1 offset to
  the param-array index so source-extracted parameter names
  align with the corresponding GOTO parameters in the bytecode.

This test exercises the instance-method case end-to-end with
three contract clauses including the `\result == x` equality that
previously crashed JBMC.
